## Subsequent distinct security-focused review

The separately required security pass was completed on 2026-08-16 and recorded in `docs/review/phase-3-p3-g1-security-review-2026-08-16.md`.

Security outcome: **changes requested; P3.G1 remains open.** Two blocking gate findings remain:

1. N-32's per-account WebAuthn assertion limiter is implemented but has no production caller.
2. N-33's per-grant recovery-attempt increment is inside the failed authentication transaction and rolls back on every refused matched grant.

The earlier “no findings” outcome above applies only to the composition-lifecycle implementation remediation. It does not supersede the security review. P3.2 is not authorized until both findings are remediated, independently re-reviewed, and accepted by the maintainer.

Both findings were accepted and remediated on 2026-08-16, recorded in `docs/review/phase-3-p3-g1-security-review-remediation-submission-2026-08-16.md`. The per-account assertion budget is now consumed by R-08 in its own committed transaction after credential resolution and before verification, with an equivalent keyed per-credential budget so the limit is not an account-existence oracle; the per-grant recovery counter is spent by R-09 through a service method that returns its refusal instead of raising it, so the increment survives the refusal it counts. Evidence is TC-BG-16 and TC-BG-17, both direct HTTP against real PostgreSQL from several source addresses, with 712 portal and 2260 bot tests passing and four falsification mutations killed.

**That remediation is submitted, not accepted.** The security review's outcome remains *changes requested* until an independent security re-review of it, and P3.G1, RAID I-09 and RAID I-10 remain open.

